+## Capture an external exchange
+
+The new local candidate's `remember` method captures an independently supplied exchange in the backend. Do not call it to copy an answer already stored by a remote Cortex run.
+
+```ts
+import type { CortexRuntimeClient } from '@cortexmemory/sdk/runtime';
+
+export async function captureExchange(client: CortexRuntimeClient, requestId: string) {
+  const captured = await client.remember({
+    conversationId: 'external-chat-1',
+    requestId,
+    userMessage: 'I play cello.',
+    agentResponse: 'Thanks for telling me.',
+    metadata: { channel: 'support' },
+  });
+  return client.rememberStatus(captured.receiptId);
+}
+```
+
+Retain the request ID before submitting. Exact replay returns the same receipt and two message IDs; changing an exchange under the same ID is a conflict. The backend verifies identity and current grants, appends individual canonical message rows and schedules its original asynchronous memory pipeline. Metadata does not grant authority, and supplied assistant text is external captured content rather than proof of a Cortex model or tool result.
+
+The receipt includes `messageIds`, `sourceEventIds`, `historyRevision`, `created`, the managed profile, `memoryStatus` and per-message fact/vector stage states. Pending indexing is an actual state; this method does not return fabricated immediate facts or vectors. `rememberStatus` performs fresh receipt/source/profile/authority checks without starting inference. Normal capture appends are separate from manual transcript edits: manual writes remain locked by default, and an administrator can explicitly lock capture appends. Existing Agent conversations keep their original edit/approval rules. These new methods require the matching backend installation. Earlier repeated-capture indexing failures remain recorded. The latest bounded cloud scenario passed capture/replay, native canonical provenance, contiguous indexing, public recall and access/lock/deletion/revocation negatives. The subsequent cloud candidate passed all four ordinary chat memory outcomes and the approved tool continuation. Subsecond latency remains unqualified; historical failures remain preserved.
